    `Father, I come to You today as the woman came to the well — with my own version of the water jar, carrying the thirst I have been trying to satisfy at the wrong wells. I bring You the five husbands of my own history — the things I have tried before You, the substitutes that promised more than they delivered, the relationships and achievements and identities I have built around the emptiness where Your water was always supposed to be. Give me the water that becomes a spring. Not better versions of what I have already been drinking. Something categorically different — something that wells up rather than runs out, that comes from inside rather than needing to be fetched, that makes me stop coming back to the same old wells because I have found the source. And let me leave my water jar. Let the encounter with You today produce in me the same forgetfulness it produced in her — the joyful, astonished forgetfulness of someone who has received something so much greater than what they came for that the original errand simply ceased to matter.`
