    `2 Kings 20 and Isaiah 38 record one of the most remarkable encounters between a human being and God in the entire Old Testament. Hezekiah is king of Judah — described in 2 Kings 18:5 as a king who trusted in the Lord, the God of Israel, so that there was no one like him among all the kings of Judah, either before him or after him. This is the king who held Jerusalem through the Assyrian siege, who saw 185,000 enemy soldiers struck down in a single night. He is not a weak man or a faithless one. He is at the height of his reign — and then the prophet Isaiah walks through the door with a sentence that stops everything: this is what the Lord says — put your house in order, because you are going to die; you will not recover from your illness. The sentence is not ambiguous. The prophet does not say you might die. He says you are going to die. The diagnosis is terminal and the prognosis is immediate. There is no treatment plan. There is no second opinion to seek. The word of the Lord, delivered by the Lord's own prophet, has announced the end. And Hezekiah turns his face to the wall and prays. The first lesson of Hezekiah's prayer is the lesson of direction. He does not turn to his physicians. He does not call his advisors. He does not arrange his funeral or begin the process of ordering his affairs. He turns his face to the wall — away from the room, away from the people, toward a space where there is nothing between him and God — and he prays. The instinct of a man who has walked with God is not to manage the crisis through human resources first and turn to God as a last resort. It is to turn toward God immediately, before anything else, because God is the first thought rather than the final one. The second lesson is the lesson of honest appeal. Hezekiah does not compose a theologically correct petition. He does not pray with the composed distance of someone who has accepted the outcome and is simply checking in. He appeals on the basis of his own walk — remember, Lord, how I have walked before you faithfully. This is not arrogance. This is the honest appeal of a man who has genuinely lived in covenant faithfulness with God and is bringing that faithfulness as part of his petition. He is not claiming to be sinless. He is saying: You have seen how I have walked. You know who I am before You. Remember that as You consider this. The third lesson is the tears. He wept bitterly. The prayer of Hezekiah is not composed. It is not dignified. It is the raw, weeping, desperate prayer of a man who does not want to die, who is not ready to die, who is genuinely devastated by the sentence he has just received. And God's response, delivered through Isaiah before the prophet has even left the middle court, includes four specific things that God says He has done: I have heard your prayer. I have seen your tears. I will heal you. I will add fifteen years to your life. The hearing of the prayer and the seeing of the tears are mentioned as separate acts of divine attention — as though both the words and the weeping were received and registered and mattered. God did not hear only the composed part of the prayer. He saw the part that had no words. The fourth lesson is the most profound: God changes outcomes in response to prayer. The sentence had been pronounced. Isaiah had delivered it as the word of the Lord. And then — before the prophet had left the building — God changed it. Fifteen years were added. The outcome that had been declared was reversed. This is not a doctrine of God being manipulated or overruled by human persistence. It is the consistent biblical testimony of a God who is genuinely responsive to His people — who builds prayer into the architecture of how things happen, who honours the cry of a person who turns their face to the wall and weeps, who can say I have heard your prayer and I have seen your tears as the immediate, specific response to an immediate, specific prayer.`
